{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T16:01:28.238Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>aa2deb068a49826ff</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_018CSrM5g6SJyKcKh11xzPC8</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/aa2deb068a49826ff.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Audit Track 1 spec quality\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Good, that's a tangent confirmation (not needed further). I have grounded all findings in actual code. Compiling the final audit.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"The spec's own api_surface entry for plan_version prescribes a brand-new fallible call — `NapiTargetsIndex::load(&amp;ws.config.groups, ws.root)?` — being wired into the real orchestration path for the first time (it does not exist in plan_version today). Reading crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs:15-60 confirms `load` returns `Result&lt;Self, ManifestError&gt;` and genuinely fails on two off-nominal inputs: an unreadable package.json (`ManifestError::Read`, e.g. permissions or a broken symlink under a napi group's package directory) and a malformed/non-UTF8/invalid-JSON package.json (`ManifestError::Parse`). `GraphError::Manifest(#[from] ManifestError)` means the `?` compiles and surfaces as `GraphError::Manifest`, but no acceptance criterion says so. AC-007 only covers pre_mutation_checks's own two error variants (FixedGroupDivergent, GroupGrammarMismatch); it is silent on what plan_version does when the newly-introduced NapiTargetsIndex::load call itself fails. A developer implementing this spec has no test obligation for that path and could let it panic, swallow it, or propagate it inconsistently with everything else.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add a new criterion: \\\"AC-007b (is_error_case: true): Given a workspace where a Fixed group's napi package.json exists at the conventional path (root/&lt;main-package-name&gt;/package.json) but contains invalid JSON (e.g. a truncated or syntactically malformed file), running commands::version::plan_version returns Err(GraphError::Manifest(ManifestError::Parse { path, format: ManifestFormat::PackageJson, .. })) where `path` is that package.json's path, and run_cascade never executes — no VersionPlan is produced. The same applies, mutatis mutandis, when the file exists but cannot be read (Err(GraphError::Manifest(ManifestError::Read { path, .. }))).\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-007\",\n      \"type\": \"incomplete\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-007 anchors its error-condition inputs to 'the same input conditions that already trigger these outcomes in pre_mutation_checks's existing unit test.' I read crates/callisto-graph/src/groups.rs in full: there is exactly one existing test for pre_mutation_checks, `pre_mutation_checks_calls_napi_drift_for_napi_groups` (line 175), and it is a happy-path test asserting a non-empty diagnostics vec — it never triggers FixedGroupDivergent or GroupGrammarMismatch. No existing test exercises either error branch (groups.rs:66-79). The criterion points a developer to a fixture that does not exist, which is exactly the kind of unverifiable-input problem the prior canon-verifier round already flagged once for fixed_group_target's test coverage — the same care was not applied here to pre_mutation_checks's claimed error-path coverage.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Replace the dangling reference with self-contained input conditions: \\\"Given a Fixed group with two released members whose base_versions are both present in `base` and both parse under the group's grammar, but whose parsed versions are unequal (e.g. member A base_versions=1.0.0, member B base_versions=1.1.0, both with a tag present in `tags`), running the real `callisto version` command (commands::version::plan_version) returns Err(GraphError::FixedGroupDivergent { group, members }) as its own Err result, and run_cascade never executes. Given instead two released members whose parsed versions use different version grammars (e.g. one SemVer, one CalVer), plan_version returns Err(GraphError::GroupGrammarMismatch { group, members }) under the same no-cascade-execution condition.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-010\",\n      \"type\": \"ambiguous\",\n      \"description\": \"AC-010/AC-011/AC-012 describe conflict detection generically as 'two group definitions' without specifying whether both must be Fixed groups, both Linked groups, or one of each. This matters because GroupTable::resolve (crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs:135-211) builds two structurally separate maps, `fixed_of` and `linked_of`, populated in two independent loops (fixed members first, linked members second) — there is no shared map today. The api_surface description ('while building the fixed_of/linked_of maps ... if a PackageId being inserted for group G2 already has an entry recorded for a different group G1') admits two valid readings: (a) conflict detection is two independent checks, one scoped to fixed_of and one scoped to linked_of, so a package legitimately in one Fixed group AND one Linked group is NOT a conflict; or (b) conflict detection is unified across both maps, so any package appearing in more than one group at all — regardless of Fixed vs Linked — is a conflict. These produce different code and different test matrices, and AC-010's own example ('group A lists my-lib, group B lists cargo:my-lib') never states whether A and B are Fixed, Linked, or mixed.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Replace AC-010 with two explicit criteria and add a disambiguating sentence to the api_surface entry. api_surface addition: \\\"Conflict detection is unified across group kinds: a single resolved PackageId may appear in at most one group total (Fixed or Linked), not one-per-kind. The check compares against a single combined view of both fixed_of and linked_of when performing the insert, not two independently-scoped maps.\\\" AC-010 (same-kind case): \\\"Given two Fixed group definitions in a workspace's raw TOML config where a member is spelled differently in each (group A lists \\\\\\\"my-lib\\\\\\\", group B lists \\\\\\\"cargo:my-lib\\\\\\\") but both spellings resolve, via the IdentityIndex, to the same real PackageId, GroupTable::resolve returns Err(GraphError::ConflictingGroupMembership { package, groups }) where `package` equals that shared resolved PackageId and `groups` contains the names of both A and B.\\\" New AC-010b (cross-kind case): \\\"Given a workspace TOML config where a resolved PackageId is listed as a member of one Fixed group and, under a different spelling, as a member of one Linked group, GroupTable::resolve returns Err(GraphError::ConflictingGroupMembership { package, groups }) naming both the Fixed and the Linked group, confirming the conflict check spans both group kinds and is not scoped separately to fixed_of and linked_of.\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": \"AC-010\",\n      \"type\": \"missing-error-case\",\n      \"description\": \"Neither AC-010, AC-011, nor AC-012 addresses the case of a single group listing the same resolved member twice under two different spellings within itself (e.g. group A lists both \\\"my-lib\\\" and \\\"cargo:my-lib\\\", both resolving to the same PackageId, with no second group involved). The api_surface wording only triggers on an entry 'recorded for a different group' — implying a same-group duplicate is not a conflict — but no criterion confirms this is a benign no-op (silent dedup via idempotent insert) rather than also an error. This is a plausible malformed-TOML input (accidental duplicate listing) that a tester cannot resolve from the current text.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"Add: \\\"AC-010c: Given a single Fixed (or single Linked) group definition in a workspace's raw TOML config that lists the same real PackageId twice under two different spellings within that one group (e.g. group A lists both \\\\\\\"my-lib\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"cargo:my-lib\\\\\\\", with no other group referencing that package), GroupTable::resolve returns Ok(..) — a duplicate spelling within one group's own membership list is not a ConflictingGroupMembership error, and `group.members` for that group contains the resolved PackageId only once (not twice).\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"criterion_id\": null,\n      \"type\": \"scope-overlap\",\n      \"description\": \"Checked against .claude/specs/SPEC-TRACK3A-WORKSPACE-MEMBERSHIP.json, SPEC-TRACK3B1-IDENTITY-PROMOTION-CORE.json, SPEC-TRACK3B2-CONSUMER-COLLISION-SAFETY.json, and track-b-idempotent-apply.json — no real overlap found. TRACK3A touches only IgnoreWalkLocator (unrelated module). TRACK3B1 touches IdentityIndex::resolve_native_with_fallback and walk.rs's collision-promotion logic; GroupTable::resolve (this spec's AC-010 target) calls IdentityIndex::resolve_human instead, a distinct function that TRACK3B1's own scope does not modify (identity.rs:355 vs :446), and TRACK3B1 explicitly states resolve_native is 'unchanged.' track-b-idempotent-apply.json operates on apply_version_plan, strictly downstream of the VersionPlan this spec's cascade/pre_mutation_checks changes produce, touching apply.rs only — no file or function overlap. This dimension passes; no issue to fix.\",\n      \"suggested_fix\": \"No fix required — confirmed no overlap.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"overall\": \"fail\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"AC-006 and AC-009's corrections both read cleanly and consistently with the rest of the spec on inspection — AC-006 correctly names plan_snapshot throughout with no residual self-contradiction, and AC-009 correctly states the binding-structure relaxation without smuggling back an implementation-detail requirement. Verified every api_surface claim (field names/counts on CascadeInput and CascadeOutcome, exact call-site line numbers in version.rs and snapshot.rs, GraphError variant field shapes, GroupTable's fixed_of/linked_of split, pre_mutation_checks's and fixed_group_target's actual bodies) directly against the source files rather than trusting the spec's prose, per the judgment standard's ban on plausible-sounding language. Two classes of issue survived that check: (1) AC-007 leans on a nonexistent 'existing unit test' fixture for pre_mutation_checks's error paths — there is exactly one existing test and it's happy-path only — so the criterion currently sends implementers to look for something that isn't there; a new, genuinely fallible call this spec itself introduces (NapiTargetsIndex::load) has zero acceptance criteria covering its failure modes even though ManifestError::Read/Parse are real, reachable outcomes once wired into the live path. (2) AC-010/011/012's conflict-detection criteria are ambiguous about whether the check spans Fixed and Linked groups jointly or is scoped per-kind, which is a real fork in the underlying data structure (two separate BTreeMaps today) and would produce two non-equivalent implementations from two competent, independent readings. A same-group duplicate-spelling input is also left unaddressed. These are the kind of near-miss, easy-to-rationalize gaps the audit exists to catch — the rest of the spec (CascadeInput wiring, raise()'s shared-target computation, snapshot.rs's no-observable-change guarantee, diagnostics merge) is precisely grounded in the actual code and passes cleanly. Overall verdict is fail until the five suggested fixes above are incorporated.\"\n}\n```\n\nFiles read/verified during this audit (all absolute paths):\n- `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/ae412d01-e061-4047-918b