{"parentUuid":"a94e903b-c0af-4a6a-8405-9f72b57bc0e6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Ce8eBrSqmgyskZ6XbRDi9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Because you chose to sequence it that way — when I found Track 2 depended on Track 1's fix, I asked how to handle it and you picked \"Do Track 1 first, then Track 2.\" Everything since then (drafting Track 1's spec, two verify/audit rounds, a failed exit-gate that caught a real architectural misdiagnosis, a full redraft, now re-verify/re-audit) *is* Track 1 — it's not separate from Track 2, it's the prerequisite work Track 2 needs before it can start safely.\n\nThe reason it's taking this long: the first exit-gate attempt caught that the spec had targeted the wrong function entirely for the core bug fix (a change to `raise()`'s sibling loop that literally cannot fire for the scenario in question — it would have shipped a spec that doesn't fix the bug). That's exactly the kind of thing this rigor is supposed to catch before any code gets written, but it does mean spec-drafting for Track 1 alone has now gone through two full drafts.\n\nWhere it actually stands: spec is redrafted correctly, currently in its second audit pass. Remaining before Track 2 can start: this audit → exit-gate (hopefully passes this time) → vector-planner (implementation plan) → vector-challenger → lambda implementation of however many tasks the plan breaks into → lambda-exit-gate → then Track 2's own spec starts from scratch.\n\nIf the pace here is a problem, I can move faster — happy to skip straight to implementation with a lighter TDD loop instead of the full pipeline, at the cost of less adversarial rigor. Want me to switch approach, or keep going as-is?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":67,"cache_read_input_tokens":404638,"output_tokens":1082,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":544