{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-17T21:35:59.734Z","sessionId":"ae412d01-e061-4047-918b-0d1df3d4d118","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a3b6b1774ff25dddd</task-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-gabe-Projects-callisto/bb531e8d-c394-4e47-a8ae-e4a9ceeca6d2/tasks/a3b6b1774ff25dddd.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Exit-gate Track 1 implementation\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Verdict: PASS\n\n```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"pass\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"blockers\": [],\n  \"verdict_summary\": \"All 16 acceptance criteria implemented and covered by real-call-path tests. 975 workspace tests pass; clippy, fmt, doc, wasm clean. cargo-mutants completed: zero survivors in changed code, 8/8 mutants in the new Fixed-group block killed. Gap: GroupTable::from_groups lacks resolve's conflict check.\",\n  \"artifact_type\": \"changeset@1\",\n  \"retry_count\": 0\n}\n```\n\nSchema-validated against `/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/orin-dx-agent-plugins/shared/schemas/verdict@1.json`.\n\n### The mutation-testing report I was given was wrong\n\nI was told lambda-mutator's run \"did not complete\" and to record it as `tool_unavailable`. That was false. The `cargo-mutants` process (PID 11538) was **still alive and running**, correctly scoped to exactly the four changed files. I let it finish rather than record a gap that didn't exist. It completed all 113 outcomes:\n\n**42 caught, 32 timeout, 10 unviable, 28 survivors — and all 28 survivors are in pre-existing untouched code**: `cascade_action` (9, explicitly Track 9's scope per the spec's non_goals), `caret_covers` (8), `coverage` (1), and pre-existing `solve_cascade` regions at lines 195-336 (10). **Zero survivors in the new Fixed-group block, version.rs, snapshot.rs, or config/groups.rs.**\n\nI checked that this wasn't vacuous — \"no survivors\" is worthless if no mutants were generated. **8 mutants were generated inside the new block (lines 384-434) and all 8 were detected.** The strongest evidence: mutants at lines 333 and 336 in the *pre-existing* Linked-group block **survived**, while their exact structural analogues at 404 and 407 in the new mirrored block **did not**. The new tests are strictly stronger than the code they were modeled on. The manual break-it-and-check verification is corroborated, not merely accepted.\n\n### Two traps I had to avoid\n\n**The exit code lied.** My first `cargo test ... | tail -60` returned exit 0 — but a pipeline returns *its last command's* status, so that 0 came from `tail`, not `cargo`. It was not evidence of anything. Re-run with `pipefail`: 361 lib tests + 22 suites, 0 failed, **0 ignored**. Same issue recurred on clippy and the workspace run, so I re-ran each capturing the true status directly: clippy exit 0 with zero warning lines, workspace **975 passed / 0 failed** across 54 suites, fmt/doc/wasm all exit 0.\n\n**Pointers are not proof.** Rather than confirm the evidence pointers resolved, I checked whether the code at each location does what the criterion requires. The AC-001 test is genuinely *discriminating*: pre-fix it yields A=2.1.0/B=0.2.0 and fails on B. AC-012's \"no assertions altered\" I verified mechanically — the entire 12-commit range is **844 insertions and exactly one deletion**, and that deletion is `ws.tags()?,`, the inline-arg replacement AC-009 mandates. I also confirmed the AC-005 test isn't vacuous: if its packages weren't discovered, `Workspace::load` would fail on `MissingGroupMember` before the assertion loop.\n\n### Gaps recorded (none blocking)\n\n1. **Sibling gap:** `GroupTable::from_groups` (`/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs:259-289`) is a third, `pub`, re-exported membership-building path with **no conflict check**. Test-only today, so not a spec violation — the spec scoped the fix to `resolve` — but it can construct a `GroupTable` violating the invariant `resolve` now enforces.\n2. **Newly-reachable pre-existing defect:** `fixed_group_target` hardcodes `SemVerVersioning`. The spec non-goals this, but wiring the block in changes its blast radius. In `plan_version` a mixed-grammar group is caught by `pre_mutation_checks` first; `plan_snapshot` deliberately does *not* call that check (AC-006), so a homogeneous all-PEP440 fixed group can now reach `SemVerVersioning.bump` and return `Err` — a failure path in `plan_snapshot` that did not exist before.\n3. **Semver-breaking public API:** `CascadeInput` gained a required field and is re-exported from publishable `callisto-graph`. No in-workspace breakage (only two production construction sites, both updated), but external consumers break.\n4. **Not run:** `moon run :audit` and the coverage-threshold gate.\n5. **Test-robustness nits:** the AC-005 test asserts a version *prefix* rather than equality to one shared version and has no non-emptiness guard; the AC-014 test asserts only `is_err()` without pinning the variant, so an unrelated failure would satisfy it.\n\nOne caveat on my own method: my parallel clippy/test runs contended for CPU during the mutation run, so the 32 timeouts are less crisply attributable than the caught/survived buckets. This does not affect the survivor count, which is what the verdict rests on.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>138714</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>80</tool_uses><duration_ms>3905205</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
